WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.640
- Big industrial inventions
in the 21st century

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.160
are made by research and development teams
at enormous corporations.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.200
They don't come out of
somewhere like this,

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.580
a traditional workshop and foundry
that can still be powered by steam

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.440
sitting in a rural town in Australia.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.620
And yet.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.560
A screw is a very simple machine,

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.600
it takes rotational motion and
turns it into linear motion

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.110
so you can rotate this screw,

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.280
the stuff down there will get picked up,

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.380
and friction will stop it
falling down again right away.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.540
That's called a vertical screw conveyor.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.920
And you'll find something
like this in factories around the world

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.820
for lifting and moving bulk
powder or grain or sand.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.080
What would happen if instead
of rotating the screw,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.560
you instead kept it still
and just rotated this cylinder around it?

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.920
My guess when someone showed me this was, well, nothing.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.360
The cylinder will just rotate in place,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.113
it's just a cylinder with a
couple of scoops at the bottom.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.090
If you're confused by that, so was I.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:08.060
- The Olds name came from Cornwall.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.980
My grandfather came out in
1868, at 18 years of age.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:18.460
Dad made his first engine,
a steam engine, in 1906 when he was 16.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.140
And 1925, my older brother was born,
he's still going,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.320
and [laughs] I came along in 1930.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.300
We served an apprenticeship
under our father, unofficially,

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.940
from when we were kids.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.580
We try to keep up with the
times as much as we can,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.850
but using traditional methods.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:42.200
Just before Christmas, I think 2003,
we had this new mixer that we'd bought.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.037
It was 20-odd thousand dollars

00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:47.036
and we had it set up where
we were going to use it.

00:01:47.036 --> 00:01:51.650
Nothing seemed suitable for
the elevation of the sand

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.200
up to the hopper over the top,
over the machine,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.310
and I woke one morning,
three o'clock in the morning,

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:01.050
with this crazy idea
and made a sketch of it

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.520
and went back to bed and went to sleep.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:04.930
And the next day I came to work

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.478
and I said, I had a
thought about making this.

00:02:07.478 --> 00:02:11.170
Nah, that won't work, they said
and I said well, we're going to try it.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.540
It was only a matter of days or a week

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.070
and we had the first
little machine operating.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.510
We drove it with an
electric drill. We set about

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:24.260
making a decent machine then
and we put it in the foundry.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:27.380
It did the job amazing. People were
coming to have a look at it

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.820
and I realised we should put a
patent application in on it.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.420
This was so different I thought
it'd be easily protected.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.750
Because rotating the
casing instead of the screw

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.800
you can see that it's different.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.797
We spent a lot of money
on patent coverage.

00:02:42.797 --> 00:02:45.950
And the name Olds Elevator
had been applied to it.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:49.480
And they're made in America and Holland
and France and Spain.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.460
[laughs] You name it.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.130
With this elevator, it's a full flow.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.850
And there's no movement of air, virtually,

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.380
except what might be between
the particles of sand.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.250
So there's really no dust
generated by the machine.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.940
Only where it falls back
through the open air.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.700
This is also a very important thing

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.070
where you're handling
dusty explosive materials.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.730
Corn dust is highly explosive.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.947
Sugar. Big explosion
in the USA a few years back

00:03:20.947 --> 00:03:24.410
and it demolished a refinery.
The clearance between

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.670
the inside of the rotating
tube and the screw

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:34.120
allows product to go through it
and not be damaged, not crushed.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.360
It's a very forgiving type of machine

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.460
because it can be in very poor condition
and it'll work.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.800
It's been found that it's a
more accurate volumetric feeder than

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.790
a lot of the devices that are being used.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.480
- This took a while to get my head around.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.820
Visually, rotating the cylinder
instead of the screw, is very different.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.560
But from a physics
perspective, it's really not.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.440
If you rotate the screw, it
looks like each level in there

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.370
is moving upwards, but
that's just an illusion.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.260
It's just rotating horizontally.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.600
Stuff gets lifted because
as the screw rotates

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.040
the particles at the top of the pile

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.150
are shoved out of the way and they prefer

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.470
to take the easy route
upwards through air,

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.577
instead of pushing the heavy
bulk below out of the way.

00:04:11.577 --> 00:04:13.860
When you rotate the cylinder,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.250
exactly the same thing is happening.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.200
The buckets underneath force
the particles to go somewhere.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.040
And when they're pushed into the screw,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.480
they take the easy route up through air.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.780
And the next ones do the same

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.270
and the next one, and the
next one, and the next one,

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.000
and everything keeps going up.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.230
They're taking the easy path.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:31.500
It is counterintuitive.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.110
And look, I am trying to
not make this be an infomercial.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.840
I checked, and yeah, this is being used
all over the world now,

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.460
from food handling to solar power towers.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.460
This is a good new way
of lifting bulk stuff.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.840
So what
I really don't understand is,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.093
through the 20th century, a century of
industrialization and automation,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.600
how did no one spot this earlier?

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.760
- I don't know why somebody
else hasn't thought of it.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.650
In the USA, they were calling me
Archimedes II. [laughs]

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:06.560
It's different, it's useful, and
if it helps people, well that's great.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.180
- Thank you to Peter Olds and all the team

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.210
at Olds Engineering. You
can find out more about them

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.160
and about the Olds Elevator at
the link in the description.

00:05:19.141 --> 00:05:23.280
I am going
to have to give you a hand

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.933
clearing up this bird seed. [laughs]

